QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "sec-eom"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What do $R_0$, $R_H$, $R_c$, $R_c'$, $\lambda$, $a$, $k$ and $d$ mean?",
     "a": r"$R_0=\beta/2\pi$ is the thermal circle's radius, an inverse temperature: larger is colder. $R_H=\sqrt{2\alpha'}$ is the flat-space type II Hagedorn value, $R_c$ the AdS$_3$ one, and $R_c'=1/R_c$ (AdS units) the BTZ's winding instability. $\lambda$ is the NS-NS fraction of the flux; small RR flux is $1-\lambda^2=a\,\alpha'/l_{ads}^2$. $k=l_{ads}^2/\alpha'$, and $d$ counts spatial dimensions, so AdS$_3$ has $d=2$.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-eom-p1", "sec-eom-p2", "sec-thermo-p5", "sec-small_RR_anal-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-flat", "at": {"node": "section-1-p3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why do string stars exist in AdS$_3$ when flat space with two spatial dimensions has none?",
     "a": r"In flat space the condensate must be bound by its own gravity, and with two spatial dimensions the equations have no normalisable solution. In thermal AdS$_3$ the thermal circle grows away from the centre, so the winding mass rises as $\rho^2$ and acts as a confining potential. That sets a size $\sqrt{l_sl_{ads}}$ even for a weak condensate; for the same reason the star cannot shrink to the string scale at lower temperatures.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-finite_RR-p1", "sec-finite_RR-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-btz-cold", "at": {"node": "sec-thermo"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why can't the AdS$_3$ string star simply turn into a small BTZ black hole?",
     "a": r"In higher dimensions small AdS black holes heat up as they shrink, like flat-space ones, so near the Hagedorn temperature a small black hole and the string star both exist and both resemble their flat-space versions. The BTZ's temperature instead grows with its radius, $R_0=1/r_h$ in AdS units: small BTZs are very cold, far below the Hagedorn temperature, so there is no small black hole for the string star to meet.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-thermo-p3", "sec-thermo-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-symmetry", "at": {"node": "sec-thermo"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why should the string star connect to the BTZ at all?",
     "a": r"The argument is from symmetry, which the paper calls a clue. Strings can wind either boundary circle, giving two $U(1)$ winding symmetries. Thermal AdS$_3$ breaks the angular one, the BTZ the thermal one, and the string star both. Exchanging the circles maps the string star to a condensate of angularly wound strings on the BTZ near $R_c'$, which also breaks both. Two branches with one symmetry pattern may meet, around $R_0\sim l_{ads}$; no calculation connects them.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-thermo-p4", "sec-thermo-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-nsns", "at": {"node": "sec-small_RR_anal"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why does NS-NS flux make the string star larger?",
     "a": r"A wound string feels two effects in AdS$_3$: the thermal circle grows towards the boundary, raising its mass, while the NS-NS flux couples to it like an electric field and lowers it. The net rise is weakened by the factor $1-\lambda^2$, so the star spreads as $(1-\lambda^2)^{-1/4}$. For pure NS-NS flux the two cancel exactly, as for long strings, and the mass is constant out to the boundary.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-finite_RR-p1", "sec-small_RR_anal-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-sign", "at": {"node": "sec-gen_disc"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Does curvature raise or lower the Hagedorn temperature?",
     "a": r"It raises it. The correction lowers $\beta_c$ (and $R_c$) below the flat-space value $\sqrt{4\pi/T_{st}}$: the winding mass grows away from the tip, so a normalisable zero mode needs a slightly smaller thermal circle, a higher temperature. The relative shift is $\frac{d}{2\sqrt2}\,l_s/l$, small when the background is weakly curved; in the D4 Witten model it is set by the glueball mass over the string tension.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-gen_disc-p5", "sec-gen_disc-p6", "sec-d4_back-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-exact", "at": {"node": "section-4.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"How reliable is the pure NS-NS Hagedorn temperature $R_c^2=2/k-1/k^2$?",
     "a": r"It comes from a leading-order effective theory, yet it agrees with the exact type II result known from the worldsheet. That agreement needs the curvature corrections to the winding mass, naively of the same order, to vanish; the paper shows they do for pure NS-NS flux by matching the exact dimension of the winding vertex operator. The zero mode is only delta-function normalisable, as in flat space, probably reflecting a continuous Lorentzian spectrum.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.2-p1", "sec-higher_curv-p1"]},
    {"id": "q-checks", "at": {"node": "sec-finite_RR"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Have the curvature corrections to the Hagedorn temperature been checked independently?",
     "a": r"Partly. In the earlier paper the analogous correction for AdS$_5\times S^5$ matched integrability. Here the pure NS-NS AdS$_3$ value matches the exact worldsheet result, and the D4 Witten model's correction also has a worldsheet derivation in other work. The finite-RR AdS$_3$ correction, of relative order $\sqrt{1-\lambda^2}\,l_s/l_{ads}$, has not been compared with recent integrability results for pure RR AdS$_3$; the paper calls this interesting.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-finite_RR-p2", "section-4.2-p1", "sec-d4_back-p3"]},
    {"id": "q-ym", "at": {"node": "sec-d4_back"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Why might the winding-string gas be related to deconfined pure Yang–Mills?",
     "a": r"At weak coupling, $\lambda_5/R\ll1$, the D4 model below energy $1/R$ is pure 3+1 Yang–Mills, which deconfines near its Hagedorn temperature, while the exchanged black brane has its own transition at high temperature. In the simplest scenario a single phase lies between, mapped to itself by exchanging $\beta$ and $2\pi R$. The winding-string gas breaks both winding symmetries, so it has that property, and the paper suggests it continues into that phase. It is a conjecture.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-d4_back-p6", "sec-d4_back-p7"]},
    {"id": "q-dominant", "at": {"node": "sec-thermo-p2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Is any of these saddles ever the dominant phase?",
     "a": r"Not where it is reliable. The AdS$_3$ string star lies above thermal AdS$_3$, its free energy being positive, and above the BTZ, which dominates near the Hagedorn temperature. The winding-string gas likewise lies above both the thermal saddle and the black brane. Only in the D4 model's weak-coupling extrapolation does the paper propose that the gas's continuation becomes dominant, between the two transitions.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-thermo-p2", "sec-small_RR_num-p5", "sec-d4_back-p5", "sec-d4_back-p7"]},
    {"id": "q-not-shown", "at": {"node": "section-1-p7"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"What does the paper not show?",
     "a": r"It constructs neither conjectured line of saddles, from string star to BTZ condensate or from winding-string gas to black brane; that would need the whole string spectrum beyond the near-Hagedorn regime, and the paper admits the AdS$_3$ evidence is weaker than in higher dimensions. Its solutions are at leading order in $\alpha'$ and in the condensate, the pure NS-NS numerics use a small RR flux as a regulator, and the relation to Yang–Mills is a suggestion.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-1-p4", "section-1-p7", "sec-small_RR_num-p1"]},
]

BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-hagedorn", "after": "section-1-p1", "title": "The Hagedorn temperature and the thermal winding mode", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The number of string states grows exponentially with energy, roughly as $e^{\beta_HE}$, so the canonical partition function diverges above a temperature $1/\beta_H$, the Hagedorn temperature. In the Euclidean path integral, temperature is a periodic time of length $\beta$, and a closed string can wind once around that thermal circle. Its mass squared is its stretching energy minus a zero-point term: for type II strings $m^2=(R^2-R_H^2)/\alpha'^2$, with $R=\beta/2\pi$ and $R_H^2=2\alpha'$. Above the Hagedorn temperature, $R<R_H$, the wound string is tachyonic, and that tachyon is the divergence. Just below it, this winding mode is a light field. Horowitz and Polchinski kept it together with gravity and found a localised condensate bound by its own gravitational attraction, the string star; the paper studies the same field in curved backgrounds.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-holo-hagedorn", "after": "section-1-p2", "title": "Hagedorn temperature and deconfinement in holography", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A large-$N$ gauge theory on a sphere has a confined phase at low temperature, with free energy of order one, and a deconfined phase at high temperature, with free energy of order $N^2$. At $N=\infty$ the density of states of the confined phase grows exponentially, like that of strings, so the confined phase has a Hagedorn temperature above which it cannot exist. In holography the confined phase is thermal AdS, and its Hagedorn temperature is where the string wound once around the thermal circle at the centre becomes massless; the deconfined phase is the AdS black hole. The first-order Hawking–Page transition between them, the deconfinement transition, happens below the Hagedorn temperature. The AdS version of the black hole/string transition is a line of large-$N$ saddles from the deconfined phase to the confined phase at the Hagedorn temperature, through the AdS string star.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-btz", "after": "section-1-p4", "title": "Thermal AdS₃ and the BTZ black hole as one solid torus", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Euclidean AdS$_3$ at finite temperature has a torus as its boundary, with a spatial circle of length $2\pi$ (in AdS units) and a thermal circle of length $\beta$. A smooth filling of the torus, a solid torus, makes one of the two circles shrink in the interior. If the spatial circle shrinks, the saddle is thermal AdS$_3$; if the thermal circle shrinks, it is the Euclidean BTZ black hole. The two are the same geometry with the circles exchanged, so the BTZ at $\beta$ is thermal AdS$_3$ at $4\pi^2/\beta$, and their actions cross at $\beta=2\pi$, the Hawking–Page transition. The exchange maps every statement about one saddle to the other: the winding tachyon of thermal AdS$_3$ at high temperature becomes, on the BTZ black hole, a tachyon of strings winding the spatial circle at low temperature. The paper's BTZ/string transition rests on this map.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hp", "after": "sec-eft-p7", "title": "How gravity binds a winding condensate", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The mass of the wound string depends on the local size of the thermal circle, $R(x)=\frac{\beta}{2\pi}\sqrt{g_{tt}}$. Write $g_{tt}=e^{2\varphi}$ in flat space. A condensate of wound strings sources $\varphi$ as a mass density sources the Newtonian potential, and the resulting negative $\varphi$ shrinks the circle where the condensate sits, lowering the winding mass there. Gravity thus lets the condensate bind itself in a potential well it digs. In flat space with $d$ spatial dimensions the equations, $\nabla^2\chi=(m^2+\kappa\varphi)\chi$ and $\nabla^2\varphi\propto|\chi|^2$ with $\kappa>0$, have normalisable solutions only for $2<d<6$, growing as the temperature approaches Hagedorn; for $d=2$ there are none. These are the Horowitz–Polchinski string stars. In AdS, or near a confining tip, the background itself makes the winding mass grow away from the centre, which is why bound condensates appear where flat space has none.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ads3", "after": "sec-eom-p1", "title": "AdS₃ with mixed RR and NS-NS flux", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Type IIB string theory on AdS$_3\times S^3\times M_4$, with $M_4$ a four-torus or K3, arises as the near-horizon geometry of strings bound to five-branes. Fundamental strings and NS5-branes support it with NS-NS flux $H_3$; D1- and D5-branes, their S-duals, support it with RR flux $F_3$. Mixed backgrounds carry both at fixed AdS radius, and the paper's $\lambda$ measures the NS-NS fraction. With pure NS-NS flux the worldsheet theory is the $SL(2,\mathbb R)$ WZW model at level $k=l_{ads}^2/\alpha'$, solvable exactly in $\alpha'$; its spectrum includes long strings, which wind near the boundary at finite energy. With any RR flux the worldsheet theory is much harder, and effective field theory or integrability must be used. A wound string couples to $H_3$ but not to $F_3$, so $\lambda$ controls how the mixed background acts on the winding mode.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-witten", "after": "sec-gen_disc-p1", "title": "Confinement from a shrinking circle: the Witten model", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Take the 4+1 dimensional gauge theory on $N$ D4 branes and put it on a circle of radius $R$, with fermions antiperiodic around it. Supersymmetry is broken, the fermions and scalars get masses of order $1/R$, and at low energies the theory is related to pure 3+1 Yang–Mills. In the gravity dual, the Witten model, the circle is a shrinking circle: it closes smoothly at a radius $u_\Lambda$, so the geometry ends there like a cigar. A string stretched along the boundary directions sits at the tip with a finite tension, the confining string, and the mass gap is of order $1/R$: this is confinement. At finite temperature there are two saddles. The thermal background, in which Euclidean time does not shrink, is the confined phase; the black brane, the same geometry with the time and $x^4$ circles exchanged, is the deconfined phase. They exchange dominance at $\beta=2\pi R$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-bf", "after": "section-4.2-p1", "title": "The Breitenlohner-Freedman bound", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In flat space a field with negative mass squared is unstable. In anti-de Sitter space it need not be: a scalar of mass $m$ in AdS$_{d+1}$ behaves near the boundary as $e^{-\Delta\rho}$, with $\Delta(\Delta-d)=m^2l_{ads}^2$, and is stable as long as $\Delta$ is real, that is for $m^2l_{ads}^2\ge-d^2/4$. This is the Breitenlohner-Freedman bound: the curvature of AdS supplies enough gradient energy to stabilise mildly tachyonic modes. In AdS$_3$ the bound is $m^2l_{ads}^2=-1$. Below it, $\Delta$ is complex and modes oscillate towards the boundary; they form a continuum and are normalisable only as delta functions, like plane waves. At the bound a mode decays as $\rho e^{-\rho}$. For the pure NS-NS winding mode, whose mass is constant in AdS$_3$, the paper finds the Hagedorn temperature exactly at this bound.</p>"""},
]
